<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # That's a Rooster! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Rooster!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Respond to classroom commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify animals by their sounds, when supported with pictures, using simple responses (e.g., 'Cats meow.' 'Dogs bark.'). Can imitate animal sounds and gestures during interactive activities (e.g., 'Moo' while pretending to be a cow). Can respond to 'What sound?' questions by mimicking the animal sound (e.g., the teacher says, 'What does the cat say?' and the student says, 'Meow!').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce the lesson's focus on animal sounds and names.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Mystery Animal Sound" game. The teacher plays an animal sound (e.g., a dog barking) and asks, "What sound is this?" while holding up a question mark flashcard. Students listen and respond chorally or individually with the sound (e.g., "Woof! Woof!"). The teacher then shows the corresponding animal flashcard (dog) and says, "That's a dog! Dogs bark." This activity uses the vocabulary: dog, bark, cat, meow, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig and the grammar 'What sound is this?' and 'The [animal] says [sound].' </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models clear pronunciation of animal names and their sounds, using flashcards and gestures. For example, the teacher points to a cat flashcard, makes a "meow" sound, and says, "The cat says meow." The teacher also models the question "What sound is this?" and the exclamation "That's a rooster!" emphasizing the /m/ sound in 'meow' and the /r/ sound in 'rooster'. The teacher uses TPR by miming animal actions (e.g., flapping arms for a bird, pretending to swim for a fish) while saying the animal names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple yes/no questions and pointing.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a cat flashcard) "Is this a dog?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Making a 'moo' sound) "Does the cat say 'moo'?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a rooster picture) "What sound does the rooster make?" (Expected answer: "Cock-a-doodle-doo!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "What's that Sound?" through an interactive presentation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An interactive digital presentation or large flashcards with clear, colorful pictures of animals (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, fish) and corresponding audio recordings of their sounds. Genre: Interactive presentation. Register: Informal, descriptive. Format: Visual and auditory input.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic: Recognizing sounds; identifying common animal sounds during listening activities (e.g., 'moo' for cow). Mimicking sounds; imitating animal sounds when prompted (e.g., 'meow' for a cat).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic: Describing animal sounds; identifying and describing different animals based on their sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of an animal (e.g., a cow) and plays its sound. Students point to the picture and say the animal's name chorally: "Cow!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a sound without showing the picture. Students listen and say, "What sound is this?" (teacher models). Then, the teacher shows two pictures (e.g., a sheep and a horse). Students point to the correct animal and try to make the sound: "Baa!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What does the duck say?" Students respond with the sound, "Quack! Quack!" (focus on vocabulary: quack).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities focused on accuracy of speaking, using target grammar and vocabulary in controlled and semi-controlled settings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: WH-questions ('What sound is this?'), Present simple ('The cat says meow.'), Exclamations ('That's a rooster!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: nouns: cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig; verbs: say; animal sounds: bark, meow, quack, woof.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: /m/ in 'meow', /r/ in 'rooster'. Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing letters (P, Q, R) in animal names like 'Pig', 'Quack', 'Rooster'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Listen and Repeat the Sound":</b> The teacher holds up an animal flashcard (e.g., "pig") and makes its sound, saying, "The pig says oink." Students repeat chorally, "The pig says oink." Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard for 'rooster' and says "The rooster says cock-a-doodle-doo." Students repeat, focusing on the /r/ sound. Next, the teacher shows 'cat' and says "The cat says meow," with students repeating and focusing on the /m/ sound. The teacher goes through all learned animals, modeling first, then inviting choral and individual repetition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Teacher Asks, Students Respond":</b> The teacher holds up an animal flashcard (e.g., "duck") and asks, "What does the duck say?" Students respond with the sound, "Quack! Quack!" The teacher then prompts for the full sentence, "The duck says quack." The teacher provides a sentence frame on the board or orally for support ("The ___ says ___."). This is repeated for various animals, encouraging simple sentence formation and sound imitation. The teacher also introduces phonemic awareness by highlighting the initial sound of 'Pig' (P), 'Quack' (Q), and 'Rooster' (R) as they practice the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Sound Match Up!":</b> Students work in pairs with smaller animal flashcards. One student picks an animal card (without showing it), makes its sound, and asks, "What sound is this?" The partner listens, identifies the animal by sound, and says its name, for example, "That's a cow!" or "Moo, moo! A cow!" They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting, and correcting pronunciation for accuracy.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Animal Sound Charades!" – a collaborative role-play / guessing game in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of animal flashcards (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from a group secretly picks an animal card. Without showing the card, they make the sound of that animal and perform a simple gesture (e.g., flapping arms for a bird, pretending to jump for a horse, moving head for rooster).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students in the group listen and watch. They take turns guessing, "Is it a cat?" or "Does it say meow?" until they identify the animal. When correct, they exclaim, "That's a [rooster]!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student who guessed correctly takes the next turn.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, providing encouragement, ensuring all students participate, and prompting with questions like "What sound is this?" or "What does the [animal] say?" to encourage full sentence responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity directly connects to the scenario "What's that Sound?" and theme "That's a Rooster!" by having students actively produce and identify animal sounds and names.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check learning and provide an opportunity for student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student participation and output during "Animal Sound Charades!" and a short "Show and Tell" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will be given one animal flashcard. They will stand up, show their card, make the animal sound, and say, "I have a [cow]. It says moo." or "That's a [rooster]! It says cock-a-doodle-doo!" if they choose to do a sound only. The teacher will prompt, "What does it say?" for students who only make the sound, encouraging the simple sentence 'It says...'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear production of animal sounds and names, especially /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Correct use of animal names (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, bird, fish) and their sounds (meow, bark, quack, woof).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Response:</b> Ability to clearly communicate the animal's sound or name when prompted.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and interaction with peers during the group activity.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I make animal sounds? (Thumbs up / Thumbs down)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I know what the rooster says? (Yes / No)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, identifying areas of success and opportunities for adjustment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral Prompts):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite animal sound to make today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to guess the animal sounds?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., The "Animal Sound Charades" game generated high engagement and oral output from students. TPR and flashcards were effective in modeling.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students needed more individual prompting for full sentence responses like "The dog says woof." I will incorporate more sentence starters or echo repetition for the next speaking task. Need to reinforce /r/ for 'rooster' with more specific pronunciation drills.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking and identifying animal sounds. For Lesson 4 (Writing), students will build on this knowledge by drawing and labeling animals, and attempting to write simple sentences about what the animals say, reinforcing phonemic awareness of letters learned (C, D, P, Q, R).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>